umbers.","Say what each number represents in the method.","A final answer alone is weaker than labelled working."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with stronger working.","Change the value or method, not only how neat it looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose fresh numbers that still use the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original working and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new numbers."]},"subject":"Measurement and Space","success_criteria":["I can use mass with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify capacity and explain why it matters.","I can practise problem independently on a new prompt.","I can notice convert needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Mass and capacity problems combine reading quantities, converting units and deciding totals or comparisons in context. Supported working follows the actual numbers and purpose of the task.","options":["Mass and capacity problems combine reading quantities, converting units and deciding totals or comparisons in context.","A calculation unrelated to the numbers given.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no working."],"question":"What should you demonstrate in this teaching stage?"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nTwo juice cartons of 250 mL total 500 mL. Three bags of 200 g flour total 600 g = 0.6 kg. Compare 1.5 L and 1200 mL → 1.5 L is greater.\n\nTRY FIRST\nSolve: 4×300 mL drinks; total mass of 5×150 g snack packs in kg and g.\n\nFEEDBACK\n1200 mL=1.2 L; 750 g = 0.75 kg.","explain":"Mass and capacity problems combine reading quantities, converting units and deciding totals or comparisons in context.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner choose methods based on place value, quantity and accurate working, rather than gue